Below is a summary of the problem and the minimal edits needed to fix it. 

1. Root cause  
   Prism’s “keyword”‐matching regex only looks behind for “not a dot” but doesn’t forbid `$`, `_`, letters or digits immediately before a keyword.  In code like  
     ```js
     const { $from, $to } = selection;
     ```  
   the “from” inside “$from” still meets the lookbehind `(^|[^.])\bfrom\b` (because `$` is not `.`), so “from” gets tokenized as a keyword.  

2. Fix  
   We must tighten the lookbehind so that it only matches at the start of input or after a character that is neither a dot (`.`) nor an identifier‐character (`[A-Za-z0-9_]`), nor the dollar sign (`$`), nor any non‐ASCII identifier characters (`\xA0-\uFFFF`).  

You will need to apply the same change in both the unminified and the minified JavaScript files.  

